WEBVTT


00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.280
In this tutorial, we'll discuss
upper arm size and set in sleeves.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:10.140
Like ready-made clothing, commercial
sweater patterns are created based on a

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.220
set of standards.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.800
What if you aren't standard size?

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.300
We covered lengthening and shortening
sleeves in a previous tutorial.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:23.880
Let's consider changing the upper arm
and the impact on the sleeve head.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:31.270
Imagine how uncomfortable this purple
sweater must be to where there's no ease

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:34.180
and the knitting is stretched
over the model's upper arm.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:40.360
This can often be an issue when it in plus
size sweaters and for men's sweaters as

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:40.690
well.

00:00:44.105 --> 00:00:49.355
The sleeve head indicated by the yellow
line must match the armhole opening.

00:00:50.075 --> 00:00:55.085
In classic dress making the sleeve head
has a bit of ease, but for this example,

00:00:55.175 --> 00:00:56.855
we'll just ignore the ease.

00:00:57.755 --> 00:01:02.015
Now what happens if you just make
the upper arm of the sleeve larger?

00:01:02.645 --> 00:01:06.965
The sleeve head is enlarged and it's
no longer going to fit in the arm hole.

00:01:07.695 --> 00:01:10.455
We don't want to alter
the depth of the arm hole.

00:01:10.515 --> 00:01:13.875
We just want to add
fullness over the upper arm.

00:01:17.524 --> 00:01:19.895
Let's picture a paper pattern.

00:01:20.645 --> 00:01:26.375
We can slash that paper pattern and spread
the upper arm to the proper dimension.

00:01:27.245 --> 00:01:31.475
Now if we stop here again, the
sleeve head's going to be too large.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:38.940
But by dropping down the top of the sleeve
head redrawing the shape will accomplish

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:44.250
what we need for our sleeve to fit, and
the sleeve head will still match the arm

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:45.090
hole opening.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:52.450
One thing to notice is that
the cuff size is also enlarged.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:57.880
If you do this, and you may not want that,
you can always alter the size of the cuff

00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:00.370
and recalculate the underarm shaping.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.540
Let's continue with the sleeve head.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:06.800
Now let's look at this another way.

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:10.009
We'll take the curves out
of the equation for now.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:16.520
The initial bind offs won't change and the
final bind offs are not going to change.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:21.500
All we're going to do is reshape the
sleeve head in the area shown by the

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:22.460
yellow line.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:32.900
Once again, we'll make
the upper arm wider.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:42.970
I'll just rotate that yellow line and it's
clear that the sleeve head's going to be

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:48.970
too large, so what we need to do
is drop that final bind off area.

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:56.565
So the length of the yellow line remains
the same and the sleeve head will fit into

00:02:56.565 --> 00:02:57.315
the arm hole.

00:03:04.515 --> 00:03:06.405
Here's another thing to notice.

00:03:06.495 --> 00:03:12.195
Not only is the upper arm of the sleeve
wider, but the shape of the sleeve head

00:03:12.195 --> 00:03:15.585
changes as well on the purple sweater.

00:03:15.585 --> 00:03:17.745
The model could use more fabric.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:19.799
Across her top of her arm.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:24.450
Changing the shape of the sleeve head
would greatly improve the fit of this

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:25.079
sweater.

00:03:28.109 --> 00:03:28.709
Okay.

00:03:28.769 --> 00:03:32.880
Now we understand the issues
surrounding modifying a set in sleeve.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.390
What we've done is fine
for a paper sewing pattern.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:41.250
You just redraw a nice curve and you're
all set as long as the sleeve had

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:43.769
measurement and matches
the armhole opening.

00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:46.470
Now what about knitting?

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:51.390
In our next tutorial, we'll talk about
different methods for drafting the sleeve

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:52.770
head for knitting.

